You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

7
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:47,040
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and my guest today is Alice Muir, Senior Growth Consultant at Feature.

8
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:56,800
Alice works across a number of product and service verticals, from health and fitness to fintech, helping apps improve their retention and CRM strategies.

9
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:09,440
On the podcast, I talk with Alice about premium user onboarding, the tactics of the life cycle optimization and why you shouldn't default to discounting for winback campaigns.

10
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,520
Hey, Alice, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

11
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:15,120
Thanks for having me, David.

12
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:17,840
Hey, we've been talking about this for such a long time.

13
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:31,840
One of the things I've said on the podcast many times before, but wanted to reiterate for those who maybe haven't heard, is that I really enjoy talking to folks like you who work with a lot of different apps and have worked with a lot of different apps over a longer period of time.

14
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:52,640
I think there's a ton of learning from founders and people who have worked at one company for ten years or whatever, but there really is a lot of learning you get from working with so many different apps where you try one strategy and it works incredibly well for one app and then it fails miserably for the next app and it really makes you think deeper about why this is working, why it's not.

15
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:58,480
I'm especially excited to have you since you do and have worked with so many different apps there at Feature.

16
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:07,040
What are some of the apps that you're currently working with or have a couple of the Adobe apps.

17
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:10,320
So I'm working with Adobe Breeder and Adobe Express.

18
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,640
I'm also working with an interesting plant health care app.

19
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:22,320
In the past, I've worked with a wide range of different industries, for example SoundCloud, the music industry.

20
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:28,240
I've also worked with Kikur, which was a French based rap music pocket studio.

21
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:32,320
And I guess in the same kind of industry, I've worked with the local.

22
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:33,440
Oh, and Headspace.

23
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,040
I worked with Headspace for a really long time.

24
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:38,160
Long time recommend this or health and wellness industry as well.

25
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:39,120
Yeah, very cool.

26
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:41,120
That's, I mean, that's quite a list there.

27
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:43,280
Lots of great learnings, I'm sure.

28
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:46,240
So there's a million different things that we could talk about.

29
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:53,280
I mean, you've learned so much, you know, working with all those different apps, but today I did want to focus on subscription life cycle strategy.

30
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:58,720
I know that that's something you've been really focused on the last few years working with so many different subscription apps.

31
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:02,000
What would you say is kind of an MVP setup?

32
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:04,160
You've got an app, it's starting to grow.

33
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:06,320
There's team bandwidth, there's resources.

34
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:11,440
to kind of put behind starting to think about subscription life cycle and user journeys.

35
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:13,200
Where would you suggest starting?

36
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:23,600
Like where's the low hanging fruit to get this set up and start actually having some wins in customer life cycle marketing and just understanding user journeys?

37
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:36,000
So I think an important MVP setup would be something like a product analytics tool to start because obviously we want to understand where the opportunity area is.

38
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:37,200
So we want to understand.

39
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:52,560
understand where the drop offs are happening in the funnel and that could be things like in the onboarding activation or specifically for subscription, it could be drop offs in the purchase funnel or people that are just not activating on the premium experience.

40
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:54,560
So that would be my number one.

41
00:03:55,040 --> 00:04:07,440
In my experience, the low hanging fruit is the strategy, the strategic life cycle targeting because you would be surprised at how many apps that we work with who are, you know, top apps on the app store.

42
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:26,080
But then when you look at the strategy and the lifecycle stages that they're targeting, they might have absolutely nothing in place for people that have started a trial or are already subscribers, where they've got loads in place for conversion, you know, onboarding, free user activation, that kind of thing.

43
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:39,280
And what I would say is that if you spend all this time converting people to a subscription, you need to make sure that you're also engaging with the people who have subscribed and that could look like.

44
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:42,560
having another onboarding for premium users.

45
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:51,120
It could look like making sure that premium users know where to go to actually use their premium features.

46
00:04:51,440 --> 00:05:03,520
A case study that we had at Feature was for an app that basically allowed people to look into investment resources and to provide financial advice.

47
00:05:04,320 --> 00:05:09,360
And there were reports, specialized reports that were available as part of the premium experience.

48
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:12,960
And they came to us and they said, you know, we just don't really understand.

49
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:19,200
We have a lot of people interested in the premium experience, but we don't have people that are staying around.

50
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,000
They might convert and then they just cancel right away.

51
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:33,120
And we looked into their data and we realized it was because the feedback that we were getting from users was around, you know, we don't actually know where to go to get these reports that we've been promised.

52
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:35,840
And so that's why cancellations were high.

53
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:40,880
And it was a really easy fix because we just needed a simple inapp message that said, Hi.

54
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:43,040
Hi and welcome to the premium experience.

55
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:48,080
Here's where you go and check out your first report or whatever and just de-link people to those reports.

56
00:05:48,240 --> 00:05:57,200
So it was actually quite a straightforward fix, but they just needed somebody to point that out to them that you know, you can't just ignore people that have subscribed.

57
00:05:57,520 --> 00:06:00,640
You need to kind of carry on communicating and engaging with people.

58
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:01,680
Right.

59
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:07,040
I hadn't really heard of that concept before of subscription onboarding.

60
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:12,160
So you always think of like onboarding to the app, but tell me about.

61
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:14,320
some of the strategies you've seen.

62
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:19,680
So once they actually start the free trial, would you at free trial start?

63
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:26,320
Is that when you would potentially start the subscription onboarding and messaging cycle or once it fully converts?

64
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:36,320
Yeah, I mean, it really depends because we've worked with apps that obviously don't have a free trial, in which case we would just say, right, let's copy out the premium user onboarding.

65
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:46,720
Obviously, if a user successfully completes the free trial, you probably don't want to resurface, you know, the same messageaging again to kind of guide them through the premium experience.

66
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:50,000
I would hope at that point that you would have had scoped out.

67
00:06:50,720 --> 00:06:58,800
It's say for example it was a seven day free trial that we would scope out seven days worth of comms or at least a strategy for those seven days.

68
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:14,320
And usually what I've said to my team as well is it's not enough just to scope out certain messaging types or a strategy, but you also need to make sure that there's a specified goal for that free user or free trial onboarding as well.

69
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:31,360
So as an example with the plant health care app that we're working with just now for the free trial, we want to make sure that users have actually had a chance to try the premium experience as part of that free trial goal.

70
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:35,840
And there's a couple of key sort of conversion events within that that we want to track.

71
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:47,680
But the main purpose is that at the end of their free trial, we can say that the users have successfully completed XYZ event, which means that they really understand what is going the value of premium.

72
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:48,480
Yeah.

73
00:07:49,200 --> 00:08:02,880
And then I guess a general approach there is kind of a feature education, but not necessarily like just teaching people how to use the app, but also kind of making sure they're experiencing the value that they're paying for, right?

74
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:13,840
When you're looking for how to build out this premium onboarding, how do you think about which features to highlight, how to highlight them, how to message them?

75
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:14,400
Yeah.

76
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:15,360
That's a really good question.

77
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:27,840
In most cases with the apps that we work with, there's usually, you know, features that are part of the free experience and then there's usually additional features that are part of the paid experience.

78
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:40,720
So what I usually try to achieve or try to explain to our clients is that we want to make sure that the guided onboarding for the premium experience is really focused on those additional features.

79
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:46,880
So the users might still perform events that they would have performed as part of the free experience, but really you want to make sure that.

80
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:56,400
the additional value that is offered is discovered by the user as part of that premium user onboarding or free trial onboarding experience.

81
00:08:56,880 --> 00:09:06,720
So with Headspace back in the days when before Headspace locked their content, there were a few things that you could do in the app and one of the main things was the basics pack.

82
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:15,120
So you could go through the basics meditation pack and that would teach you key methods for mindfulness and meditation.

83
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:26,480
But then if you upgraded to premium you could actually choose certain packs based on issues that you were having in your life or key areas that you wanted to focus.

84
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:34,400
So you could choose, for example, a stress pack or there were things like mindful eating, I think was one of them.

85
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:36,240
There was also sleep as well.

86
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:48,240
And so what I would recommend is that if a user had become premium at that point that you surface perhaps an in-app message survey that says, Welcome to the premium experience.

87
00:09:48,960 --> 00:10:01,760
Can you let us know what features you were interested in, what brought you to premium and then kind of guiding users and adding that extra bit of personalization to make sure that they activate on the thing that they're paying for, if that makes sense?

88
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:03,200
Yeah, yeah, totally.

89
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:06,720
And then how do you think about the blend of the different channels?

90
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:09,520
So you've been talking a lot about in app messaging.

91
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:14,240
How do you think about also kind of following up with email and push notifications?

92
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:25,200
And is that something where these tools like Braze are going to push the message when someone's in the app, but if they haven't opened the app in a certain number of days, then it would go via push or via email.

93
00:10:25,520 --> 00:10:35,440
And is that kind of part of the flow of creating these if and statements of, you know, if they don't open or, you know, in sequencing that way or how do you think about that blend of channels?

94
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:37,440
Yeah, we get asked this quite a lot.

95
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:38,720
It's a really good question.

96
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:44,640
I think from my experience, email is really good for running things like drip campaigns.

97
00:10:44,960 --> 00:10:50,160
So what we mean by drip campaigns is maybe offering one discount at the beginning.

98
00:10:50,320 --> 00:11:14,880
So if you have tracked in the data the average time that it takes for a typical user to upgrade to Premium, say it's seven days from downloading the app, and you've got a segment of users who are not upgrading to Premium within those seven days, then what you could do is send an email campaign that said, you know, I'm going to give you, for example, a ten percent discount.

99
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:15,840
You start off small.

100
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:29,840
And then if that user still hasn't upgraded within two days later, five days later, maybe a week later, then you can start to slowly increase the discount percentage and that's what we would call a drip campaign.

101
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:34,640
That's something that headspace did back in the early days of me working with them.

102
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:40,720
So we're talking maybe like two, three years ago, that's what they did and they saw some good results from that.

103
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:48,560
I don't know if they're still doing that to date, but that's where email is really good and I think email is really good for long form content as well.

104
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:54,640
So keeping premium users engaged if you've got an app that sort of fits into your lifestyle.

105
00:11:55,040 --> 00:12:10,000
I think Headspace again is a good contender for that because you can also send long form content about how to use mindfulness or meditation within your daily life, for example, and just keeping people engaged with the concepts of mindfulness.

106
00:12:10,720 --> 00:12:20,160
I think push has its limitations because as we all know, Apple has certain rules around what you can actually send in a push notification.

107
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:23,920
And also on top of that, you don't want to be really stubborn.

108
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:33,680
really spammy and try to kind of push sales on people's home screens because I think that's probably a recipe for somebody and unsubscribing from your push notifications.

109
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:46,800
So I would say push is better for, as you alluded to earlier, win back scenarios and kind of reminding people that they're there or that they need to complete an action or just reminding people to come back and try something.

110
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:47,760
Yeah.

111
00:12:47,920 --> 00:13:04,880
And then in addition to that, push notifications are becoming a harder and harder channel with Iowa 16 where I'm missing more and more even just text messages now because of the way Apple changed up the you have to go into settings to revert it back to the old way of showing all your notifications on the screen.

112
00:13:05,040 --> 00:13:07,120
And I doubt many users have done that.

113
00:13:07,520 --> 00:13:16,560
I don't know if you've seen this in the data at all, but it certainly feels like it's less and less reliable as a way to reach people in complement to that.

114
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:20,080
What strategies do you think work better with in-app messaging?

115
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:29,120
And then what styles of in-app messages are you working with, like dropping down from above or like in a feed or like, yeah, tell me about how you think about in-app messaging.

116
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:36,240
I love that question because it ties in with what we talked about before around the flexibility with in-app messages.

117
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:45,200
We typically want to choose a tool that enables us to create custom in-app messages so that we can play around with the formats and stuff.

118
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:51,760
We've had situations where we've created full screen in-app messages so that they look like native paywalls.

119
00:13:52,320 --> 00:14:00,240
And I think this is a really interesting topic because people talk a lot about, you know, paywall testing tools and how to go about paywall eB testing.

120
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:10,720
But I haven't personally, I haven't seen a lot of information in the industry around using inap messages as a kind of proxy for testing paywalls.

121
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:14,880
It is possible, but it does have a lot of drawbacks as well.

122
00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:21,600
For example, the fact that depending on your setup, inap messages are reliant on the server's loading.

123
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:37,520
And so sometimes there's glitches here and there where not everyone receives the inap message., in which case if it was a crucial paywall that was early in your onboarding, for example, you're probably going to want to have that as a native solution just to ensure visibility.

124
00:14:38,400 --> 00:14:54,720
So that is one thing, you know, having the full screen versions, but we've also, and I think we might have talked about this in a previous webinar as well, but we've also tested like banner formats that have had really surprising conversion results and then, you know, interspatial formats.

125
00:14:55,360 --> 00:15:00,720
We've tested things as well, like having Annika who works on BLayer at Feature.

126
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:03,200
BLayers are in app messaging tool.

127
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:06,720
She has created things like scratch cards functionality.

128
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:16,080
So you know, you could almost like gamification within the in app messages and also, you know, being able to include like videos, things like that.

129
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:24,880
I think it was Jake from Superwall as well who said that video paywalls and video tutorials in paywalls perform really, really well.

130
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:28,720
So that's something that we've wanted to test in house as well.

131
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:34,400
So those are some examples of different types of formats and ways of approaching inadmissible.

132
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:36,800
It just shot at me while you were talking.

133
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:40,880
I've been seeing that a lot in my FitnessPal specifically.

134
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:53,840
So I subscribe, I've been subscribed off and on to my FitnessPal probably for like six or eight years and I'll track my diet really hardcore for a few weeks or a month and then I'll have a good sense of what I'm doing.

135
00:15:54,080 --> 00:16:06,240
And so I was on the annual plan for a little while and then now I was'm a free user, but there's still like a lot of freemium functionality that I can still use.

136
00:16:06,560 --> 00:16:07,760
I still use it every now and then.

137
00:16:08,560 --> 00:16:13,200
I've been getting these like full screen intersticial that like either lead to a paywall.

138
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:21,200
Then I thought it was really interesting the other day, one came up that was just like a did you know having an accountability partner, yada yada yada.

139
00:16:21,520 --> 00:16:26,960
It was kind of a call to action to take you to a part of the app that I had never been to before.

140
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:35,040
I think that's a really good example of what you were talking about, of kind of combining this feature education with a full screen.

141
00:16:35,520 --> 00:16:45,520
In this particular case, it was actually more like a pop up where it took over just like a quarter of the screen and looked like a notification with the rest of the app darkened behind it.

142
00:16:45,760 --> 00:16:56,720
It seems like it can be a really effective way to kind of drive engagement to the parts of the app that maybe someone hasn't used before that you know is a really valuable part of the app.

143
00:16:57,120 --> 00:17:06,960
Are there other specific exampl scenarios where it's been very successful in driving adoption or retention?

144
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:10,160
First of all, I absolutely love my FitnessPal.

145
00:17:10,320 --> 00:17:12,960
I found it a great feature that I'm obsessed with.

146
00:17:13,280 --> 00:17:17,280
I think I've been retained on that app for about three years or something.

147
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:33,440
But what's interesting, before I answer about the inapp messages, what's interesting about my FitnessPal is that I get so much value out of the free experience that I'm surprised that they get anyone to convert to premium because I just find honestly the free.

148
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,480
experience does everything I needed to do and more.

149
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:44,000
So a great example of an app that has a growing retention strategy for in terms of messages and innovative things that we can do.

150
00:17:44,320 --> 00:17:52,960
We did a project recently where we worked with a dating app that targeted or at least had pricing that was split by local.

151
00:17:54,000 --> 00:18:01,440
So that was an interesting challenge for us because this dating app didn't have an eB testing tool to work with, right?

152
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:04,800
So in terms of an eB testing payball tool..

153
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:20,560
They had a CRM tool so we had to work with in-app messages and it was an interesting challenge because we wanted to run a bunch of tests on paywall and conversion messaging, but we had to work with the fact that there were different prices based on region.

154
00:18:21,680 --> 00:18:32,480
So my colleague Alejandra, she actually did a lot of research on how can we pull dynamic links or dynamic price points into in-app messages.

155
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:36,160
She's written an article that I think is on the feature blog about this.

156
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:39,680
Her name is Alejandra Escobal, if anyone wants to google that.

157
00:18:40,320 --> 00:18:44,640
And what we were able to do is create one inap message.

158
00:18:44,960 --> 00:18:50,320
So one inap message design and sort of template or structure or whatever.

159
00:18:51,040 --> 00:18:57,600
And then we would set that up as one individual campaign and based on the attribute that the user had.

160
00:18:57,840 --> 00:19:01,280
So based on their region, the price would dynamically change.

161
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:14,320
So it's quite funny because I think inap messages can sometimes be overlooked as paywall testing tools, but actually they're really, really powerful and quite flexible, and we had some pretty good success with that.

162
00:19:14,640 --> 00:19:26,160
I think one limitation is that if you run a campaign like that, you can't split the results by region, you would just get aggregate results, and that's obviously not ideal in all scenarios, so there are limitations of it too.

163
00:19:26,880 --> 00:19:35,120
Yeah, so that's a really cool way to take a tool intended for one thing and kind of hack it into achieving the results and being able to test what you want to test.

164
00:19:35,520 --> 00:19:45,680
We've kind of already delved into some much more sophisticated strategies, but I did want to get a sense for when a client comes to you and they have the basics covered.

165
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:58,880
What are those kind of next steps to really find and uncover the opportunities as an app grows to get more and more sophisticated about life cycle messaging and these sorts of things?

166
00:19:59,200 --> 00:19:59,280
Yeah.

167
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:12,720
So for apps that are really advanced in their strategy that run, you know, hundreds of AB tests a year, I think the biggest thing that we can do is usually apps that are at that stage have a lot of data.

168
00:20:13,600 --> 00:20:19,120
And so it means that we can kind of go into the data and pull correlation analysis.

169
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:28,640
So we can look at specific features or a specific path that users take that correlates with becoming a converted user or a premium user.

170
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:39,360
And that kind of analysis or that type of work is really difficult for early stage startups or early stage apps because there's just not the data there to be sure about correlation, if that makes sense.

171
00:20:39,920 --> 00:21:00,480
So I think that that's probably one of the best or one of the biggest areas of opportunity is really, you know, how can we create segments of high converting users and then find segments of people that replicate aspects of that behavior and we can kind of push them more towards the specific journey that does eventually correlate with conversion.

172
00:21:00,960 --> 00:21:02,240
So that would be one thing.

173
00:21:02,880 --> 00:21:22,640
And I think also oftentimes people that are starting out or apps that are quite new, they tend to have., maybe even if they have one payball at the onboarding, they tend to kind of bury their payballs and kind of bury their premium messaging or conversion messaging in, you know, deep into the journey.

174
00:21:22,880 --> 00:21:29,280
So that's also something that usually for clients that come to us and they're looking for us to pick holes in their strategy.

175
00:21:29,600 --> 00:21:40,720
I usually say to people, if the user doesn't see the opportunity to convert, they don't see a payball, they don't receive any messaging about that, then there's a 100% chance that they're not going to convert.

176
00:21:41,360 --> 00:21:49,600
So, you know, sometimes it's more obvious things like that, and other times it's more about, well, you know, we've got a huge amount of data now, how can we actually leverage that data?

177
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:54,000
What does that correlation analysis actually look like?

178
00:21:54,320 --> 00:22:04,000
Are you doing that in Amplitude or is that a data warehouse situation where you're writing custom queries or how do you actually cover those kind of optimal journeys?

179
00:22:05,040 --> 00:22:11,520
So Amplitudes or MexPanel are great tools for being able to access that type of data.

180
00:22:12,480 --> 00:22:20,080
Not all clients have a setup like that, so sometimes we do have to write a data brief or write some queries or things like that.

181
00:22:20,720 --> 00:22:33,200
But basically what we would do is we would analyze key events that are part of the core functionality and we would basically then pull together what is essentially called a correlation analysis.

182
00:22:33,360 --> 00:22:42,320
So we would I think in amplitudes there's a score that's assigned to certain events to see how likely they are to correlate with another event.

183
00:22:43,040 --> 00:22:54,480
So in that case, you would say, right, how likely is it that completing X meditation pack, for example, correlates with somebody becoming a premium subscriber?

184
00:22:54,960 --> 00:23:00,400
And you would get Amplitude would actually be able to show you a score that shows you that likelihood, if that makes sense.

185
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:13,840
As growth marketers, it's much easier to have a tool like Amplitude, for example, because you can sell, serve, but that correlation analysis does get a bit complex if you have to have your analysts use the data warehouse or something like that.

186
00:23:14,720 --> 00:23:14,800
Yeah.

187
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:19,840
I mean, what do you typically look at for endpoints that you're trying to correlate to?

188
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:39,520
So you specifically set like the subscription conversion event or a certain number of renewals as like your primary metric and then you're looking kind of back down the funnel for correlation or do you even do that with like onboarding completion and then look at correlation to just events that lead up earlier in the onboarding?

189
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:44,880
Usually, I mean, onboarding completion could be one potentially with software.

190
00:23:44,880 --> 00:23:56,720
With stuff like that though, what I struggle with people that are new to growth marketing or people that are new to the team is sometimes we pull analysis like that and it's kind of a bit stately obvious.

191
00:23:56,800 --> 00:24:05,360
So obviously we know that if someone but it's a funny thing because it's a if you're not an analyst or you're not trained in that way, you take someone to point that out to you.

192
00:24:05,840 --> 00:24:12,800
But things like, you know, if someone registers then they're already showing a high intention with the product, right?

193
00:24:13,520 --> 00:24:20,800
So we know that if someone registers that their intention is already high and they've put time, especially if the app has a non-mandatory registration.

194
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:29,360
So we could already make a guess that that segment of users who have chosen to register are good candidates for an upsell.

195
00:24:29,600 --> 00:24:46,320
When we're doing the correlation analysis, it's usually things like if we use the Pocket Studio example, people who have entered the coins in the studio and they've used a particular filter, for example, or like particular editing, advanced editing feature.

196
00:24:47,360 --> 00:24:58,080
Those users are more likely to convert to subscription and usually the reason around that is that you know they have a need or a high intention to use professional editing features.

197
00:24:58,560 --> 00:25:03,760
And then once you kind of have those correlations, what are the practical steps?

198
00:25:03,920 --> 00:25:16,720
So you specifically build an inapp message pointing people to use that or an e-mail campaign about the value of editing and how it's going to improve your business or make you an influencer star or something like that.

199
00:25:17,360 --> 00:25:24,160
Once you've done the correlation analysis, what are the practical steps to actually get value and drive those outcomes?

200
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:46,080
That's the really fun part because when we run an audit for a client that we're just new with or if I was to kind of dive into an app that had been approached by, I would first of all spend time building the business case and the audit and the storyline of what or the timeline of what the next six months to twelve months look like.

201
00:25:46,560 --> 00:25:48,640
And that's when you would have things like your correlation.

202
00:25:49,280 --> 00:25:51,520
analysis, you would have funnel analysis.

203
00:25:51,760 --> 00:25:54,560
You're looking at different scenes to understand where people drop off.

204
00:25:55,200 --> 00:26:03,440
At that point, once you kind of have finished building out the timeline and the scope, then you move into right, okay, so we know we've got these drop offs.

205
00:26:04,080 --> 00:26:07,200
How can we kind of fill those gaps or bring people back in?

206
00:26:07,680 --> 00:26:22,320
And exactly as you said, would an email drip campaign work if we see that there's a segment of users that look like they have a certain amount of conversion potential, would it just take a 10% discount here just to kind of nudge them in the right direction.

207
00:26:22,560 --> 00:26:25,200
And I think that's what CRM is all about really.

208
00:26:25,520 --> 00:26:37,920
It's just about giving people that extra nudge or that extra push in the right direction of the behavior that you want them to take, keeping in mind natural behaviors and natural frequency and things like that as well.

209
00:26:38,240 --> 00:26:41,600
So a good example is a period tracking app, right?

210
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:46,880
You might work in the CRM department or work for a client like that.

211
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:50,480
There's no way that you're going to get users to.

212
00:26:50,640 --> 00:26:56,640
to use the app from a monthly to a daily frequency because it requires science, right?

213
00:26:57,040 --> 00:27:06,720
So it's it's all about nudging people in the right direction, the behaviors you want them to take, but making sure that it's actually a realistic strategy with the natural frequency of use, if that makes sense.

214
00:27:07,680 --> 00:27:13,040
We've already started to touch on it, but the next thing I did want to talk about is churn prevention strategies.

215
00:27:13,520 --> 00:27:17,760
I think when a lot of people think CRM, the very first thing they think is winbacks.

216
00:27:18,080 --> 00:27:21,840
And of course, we've already kind of explored so many different ways.

217
00:27:23,040 --> 00:27:27,920
that a CRM tool can provide so much more value than just winbacks, but winbacks are really important things.

218
00:27:28,160 --> 00:27:38,320
So I'd love to kind of hear some of your success stories and tactics behind how you think about winbacks and what kind of results you're able to see in optimizing those strategies.

219
00:27:38,960 --> 00:27:39,520
I agree with you.

220
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:51,200
I think that a lot of people talk about quick wins and winbacks, but actually the way that I would describe the approach that I take is more holistic and kind of long term looking at long term success.

221
00:27:51,840 --> 00:27:58,080
I think someone that you've had on the podcast previously who talked about this a lot as well is Robbie Killman Baxter.

222
00:27:58,880 --> 00:28:13,600
I've noticed that I thought the podcast episode with Robbie Killman Baxter was brilliant and I've kind of listened to or read other things that she's written and she always makes a great point about the fact that you need to be constantly creating value for the user.

223
00:28:14,240 --> 00:28:20,480
It's not just about a user that's thinking of turning and at that point you kind of step in and say hey come back.

224
00:28:21,040 --> 00:28:27,120
It's more about making sure that throughout the subscription that you're continuing to add value to people's lives.

225
00:28:27,680 --> 00:28:36,240
And the way that I do that through CRM is, I would say, similar to as we talked about before, I would look at the different journeys that are in place.

226
00:28:36,720 --> 00:28:56,960
And if a publisher that I'm working with is spending a lot of time converting people to a subscription but then completely ignoring them for the entirety of their subscription, then I would kind of, which is really common, I would kind of step in and say, maybe we could add some quick wins and win bad strategies at the end of the funnel.

227
00:28:57,440 --> 00:28:57,600
Sure.

228
00:28:58,240 --> 00:29:01,040
But I think it's kind of slapping a band-aid on the problem.

229
00:29:01,840 --> 00:29:19,360
And I think this is really topical at the moment because obviously we're in the middle of a recession or a cost of living crisis in some places and people are they're looking at what's coming out of their bank account and people are scrutinizing products more than they would have been in the past.

230
00:29:20,160 --> 00:29:22,400
I always use the example of Netflix.

231
00:29:22,880 --> 00:29:25,520
I think that for I mean, I live in Germany, so maybe it's just.

232
00:29:25,920 --> 00:29:26,720
the German content.

233
00:29:27,120 --> 00:29:33,440
But for me, I think Netflix has really slowed down in terms of the content that is available that I want to watch.

234
00:29:33,920 --> 00:29:40,880
And so I'm not the only person I've spoken to recently that's considering even canceling a Netflix subscription for that reason.

235
00:29:41,520 --> 00:29:52,720
It's kind of a mixture of making sure that you're engaging with people throughout the subscription, but also making sure that you're continuing to provide new content, you know, adding new value.

236
00:29:53,040 --> 00:29:55,680
I think Headspace is a great example of.

237
00:29:55,840 --> 00:30:14,880
of an app that has been quite creative in how it deals with this because with Headspace, once someone knows how to meditate, all they really need is a stopwatcher, a clock on their phone that they can then say, right, I need three minutes of silence and that's what they're competing with.

238
00:30:15,520 --> 00:30:23,520
But Headspace have been really smart and, you know, over the years in that they've then said, right, well, how can we bring mindfulness to exercise?

239
00:30:24,480 --> 00:30:26,480
Or how can we bring mindfulness to sleep?

240
00:30:26,560 --> 00:30:36,080
And it's adding new use cases and continuing to add value so that people are not getting bored of the core experience or the core value proposition.

241
00:30:36,720 --> 00:30:42,400
Yeah, that's great pointing people back to the value and making sure that you continue delivering on the value.

242
00:30:42,640 --> 00:30:50,880
And, and, you know, you alluded to Robbie Kellman Baxter, the subscription is the start of your relationship with them, not the end of your relationship.

243
00:30:51,200 --> 00:30:53,200
The end goal is not for them to subscribe.

244
00:30:53,360 --> 00:30:58,240
That's the start of the long term retention and with the revenue cat.

245
00:30:58,720 --> 00:31:03,520
annual report that we published earlier this year, you know, consumer subscriptions, it's tough.

246
00:31:03,760 --> 00:31:08,160
The median retention at one year for an annual subscription is somewhere around twenty five percent.

247
00:31:08,400 --> 00:31:15,600
And so finding wins in retention can start to compound long term and really build a much better business.

248
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:22,960
You know, if you're churning out seventy five percent of your users at the end of one year, it's really tough to build a solid business.

249
00:31:23,280 --> 00:31:25,600
You're just constantly having to bring in new users.

250
00:31:25,840 --> 00:31:34,480
So these strategies and applying them and thinking in this way, I think., can have a huge impact long term, but even in the short term, just continuing to get that revenue in.

251
00:31:34,880 --> 00:31:41,760
I think that's why my Fitness PAL has such a good retention strategy because it really taps into the user's psychology.

252
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:50,080
I think at the end of every day, when you log your vid diary, it says, if every day was like today, here's where you would be in five weeks.

253
00:31:50,800 --> 00:31:54,800
And so it really kind of keeps people, again, that's part of the free experience.

254
00:31:54,960 --> 00:31:58,400
It's not part of the paid experience, but it really keeps people engaged.

255
00:31:58,640 --> 00:32:03,440
It taps into the psychology of goal setting and future planning and things like that.

256
00:32:03,600 --> 00:32:09,280
And I think the fact that it's so personalized as well makes it a really, really good strategy.

257
00:32:09,600 --> 00:32:17,520
But I think the other good thing that they do in relation to this topic is that they also have really good blog content that's constantly updated.

258
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:24,080
I think I've seen things like recipes on there as well, different kinds of exercises to try and all that kind of stuff.

259
00:32:24,240 --> 00:32:29,280
So you're constantly engaged not only with your own progress but also with the content that's in the app.

260
00:32:29,600 --> 00:32:30,480
And I think that's really important.

261
00:32:30,880 --> 00:32:30,960
Yeah.

262
00:32:31,120 --> 00:32:45,360
Yeah, we already started to answer it, I guess, but then the next question I was going to ask you was around spotting and then re engaging users before they actually churn, both kind of engagement churn as we've been discussing, but then also like fully churn and canceling the subscription.

263
00:32:45,600 --> 00:32:51,840
So in addition to what we've already discussed, are there specific ways to spot that churn before it happens?

264
00:32:52,480 --> 00:32:57,200
And then like how do you think about spotting the churn and then finding ways to re engage those users?

265
00:32:57,680 --> 00:32:57,760
Yeah.

266
00:32:58,240 --> 00:33:04,320
So we would normally segment users that we considered to have started to disengage.

267
00:33:05,120 --> 00:33:10,480
So in order to do that, you need to understand what your expected frequency of usage is to begin with.

268
00:33:10,880 --> 00:33:12,880
Say that's weekly, right?

269
00:33:13,520 --> 00:33:18,960
And then you've got a segment of users that are maybe using the app every two weeks or every three weeks.

270
00:33:19,680 --> 00:33:22,000
Then you're kind of thinking they're still using the app.

271
00:33:22,240 --> 00:33:25,920
They obviously still have a need for it, but they're kind of starting to drift.

272
00:33:26,080 --> 00:33:27,360
They're starting to disengage.

273
00:33:28,320 --> 00:33:30,000
And there can be a number of different reasons.

274
00:33:30,160 --> 00:33:34,800
It doesn't always mean that the person doesn't want to use your products anymore.

275
00:33:35,360 --> 00:33:40,400
There can be a mix of external and internal factors that can influence that.

276
00:33:40,800 --> 00:33:50,080
For example, if we go back to an app that we worked with in the past, a period tracking app, we ran a survey with them with their disengaging users.

277
00:33:50,720 --> 00:33:58,480
It sounds really obvious, but we figured out that there was a segment of users that actually were pregnant and so they didn't need to use the app.

278
00:33:58,640 --> 00:34:02,480
I mean, it sounds so obvious when you think about it, but I am a blowing the hold.

279
00:34:02,880 --> 00:34:10,080
We still, you know, we still have had to go and uncover that, but there was a segment of users that obviously didn't need to use the app in the same way.

280
00:34:10,640 --> 00:34:15,520
And so the product that we were working with had to find a way to keep those people engaged.

281
00:34:16,000 --> 00:34:21,840
Now luckily it had a strong brand and was well known for women's health research.

282
00:34:22,320 --> 00:34:28,800
And so a lot of people would still stay around and snore neon as frequently because they liked the content.

283
00:34:29,040 --> 00:34:31,280
It was like medical research and things like that.

284
00:34:31,920 --> 00:34:38,320
But that would have been a prime example of an app that needed to also be quite creative in adding new use cases.

285
00:34:38,800 --> 00:34:48,960
There can also be other things that unfortunately are just out with the publisher's control, things like competition with other apps but also with native competition as well.

286
00:34:49,600 --> 00:35:00,640
For example, I think the Apple Health app has evolved really nicely over the last few years, which means that apps like period trackers and even Leroy's are running calorie trackers.

287
00:35:01,120 --> 00:35:08,080
There is more competition because people are like, why would I pay for that when I get it for free or a version of it for free on my phone.

288
00:35:08,720 --> 00:35:13,280
So these are external factors that we have to be creative and work alongside.

289
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:14,080
Yeah.

290
00:35:14,320 --> 00:35:27,360
So then how, as an example with the period tracking app, once you've covered that thing, okay, while they're pregnant and then even into breastfeeding, I mean, my wife and I have had four children, so I'm pretty aware of how all that goes.

291
00:35:28,000 --> 00:35:31,360
It can be months before they start having a period again if they're breastfeeding.

292
00:35:31,520 --> 00:35:31,600
Yeah.

293
00:35:32,000 --> 00:35:39,840
And so did you actually build a specific campaign around pregnancy health and then target a re engagement in the future.

294
00:35:40,320 --> 00:35:48,960
And then how would those kinds of strategies maybe play out in different apps where you find specific cohorts and specific reasons behind potential churn or engagement churn?

295
00:35:49,440 --> 00:35:57,440
So for this particular project, it was more about doing a bit of opportunity scoping and building out a business case study or a timeline for the next few months.

296
00:35:57,600 --> 00:36:03,840
So that was something that we kind of said to the we to make sure that you're still leveraging content for those specific users.

297
00:36:04,320 --> 00:36:09,440
Unfortunately, I haven't had the chance to actually try and win those types of users back.

298
00:36:10,080 --> 00:36:16,560
I think it would be really difficult, you know, and even in the space of time just because obviously we do now have the Apple Health.

299
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:21,600
I'm on iOS, which is why I always say Apple, but I'm sure Android has an equivalent as well.

300
00:36:21,920 --> 00:36:23,120
That's the tricky thing, isn't it?

301
00:36:23,280 --> 00:36:26,880
Because then you're talking nine, ten months plus out of use.

302
00:36:27,760 --> 00:36:33,120
And as we all know about habits, it takes fifty days at least to kind of build a habit again.

303
00:36:33,440 --> 00:36:43,760
So then my suggestion there would be you need to think about the users that are stuck around, but maybe at the beginning of being able to come back and really think about habit reinforcement.

304
00:36:44,320 --> 00:36:46,640
And that could even look like re onboarding users.

305
00:36:47,040 --> 00:36:51,200
It could look like bringing people back, reminding them why they signed up in the first place.

306
00:36:51,520 --> 00:36:52,640
Those types of things are good.

307
00:36:52,960 --> 00:37:02,000
I think with Headspace, for example, at some point we tested a commitment campaign where we said to people, here are the reasons why you joined Headspace.

308
00:37:02,560 --> 00:37:08,640
And then we would kind of resurface that later to say, like, remember, you wanted to reduce stress, you wanted to do this, that, next thing.

309
00:37:08,960 --> 00:37:10,080
And that was quite effective.

310
00:37:10,480 --> 00:37:15,120
way to remind people and sort of reinforce the habit as well, which can be very challenging.

311
00:37:15,680 --> 00:37:38,480
And then lastly, as we're wrapping up, I mean, we both kind of talked about it not being the end all be all of CRM, but are there specific winback strategies that you have seen work well, you know, when someone does actually turn off the free trial or they've been subscribed for a year on an annual plan or a few months on a monthly plan and then they hit that turn off auto renew button.

312
00:37:38,960 --> 00:37:40,480
What's some of the low hanging fruit there?

313
00:37:41,120 --> 00:37:46,640
or interesting experiments you've seen be successful in winning back some of those users who've churned.

314
00:37:47,280 --> 00:37:54,640
So some really cool campaigns that I've seen at Feature are things like reminding people of the features that they're going to lose.

315
00:37:54,880 --> 00:38:05,120
I worked with VISCO a couple of years ago and that was something that we were able to do was to sort of remind people of the benefits that they lose when they don't have membership anymore.

316
00:38:05,840 --> 00:38:09,840
Reminding people as well or creating a sense of urgency around that.

317
00:38:10,080 --> 00:38:11,520
So saying you've got.

318
00:38:12,160 --> 00:38:16,080
seven days left of this particular filter, this kind of thing.

319
00:38:16,880 --> 00:38:29,680
And another one as well that worked both in terms of conversion and for people that had cancelled was something really cool that we had done for a creative app was we implemented screenshot capture functionality.

320
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:42,720
So if someone had tried to screenshot something creative that was actually a premium feature that they could test out, we would say to them, look, this is actually better in high resolution or is it better?

321
00:38:42,720 --> 00:38:48,240
Or it's a photo if you want to be able to download this, you know, and it was almost like a bit of a guilt trip.

322
00:38:48,320 --> 00:38:51,440
It was kind of tapping into psychology and that worked really nicely as well.

323
00:38:52,160 --> 00:39:10,640
But I think, you know, reminding people of what they're going to miss out on, reminding people of their usage and features that they have enjoyed, or I think with another photography app that we worked with, we were able to resurface images that they'd created in the past as a kind of celebration of their membership.

324
00:39:11,040 --> 00:39:12,160
So we did that as well.

325
00:39:12,240 --> 00:39:12,880
It's cool.

326
00:39:13,200 --> 00:39:13,280
Yeah.

327
00:39:13,680 --> 00:39:21,120
So you can get really creative with these things, but the basic principles remain the same, just reinforcing the value proposition, I would say.

328
00:39:21,680 --> 00:39:33,040
So the one thing you didn't mention was discounting, which I think is really interesting, but kind of speaks to your mindset, is that discounts are kind of the obvious, like everyone try, like, yeah, hey, forty percent off.

329
00:39:33,200 --> 00:39:42,800
I mean, even Headspace, I've been a subscriber to Headspace, and just in the last week I got an email like, come back, forty percent off, you know, seven days only if you resubscribe or whatever.

330
00:39:43,120 --> 00:39:44,720
I imagine you still use discounts.

331
00:39:45,360 --> 00:39:52,000
But how have you seen that blend or do you even use both at the same time of like reminding people of the value and offering them a discount?

332
00:39:52,480 --> 00:39:58,800
But what's kind of the blend you see of like things really performing well, just discounting or just value or both?

333
00:39:59,360 --> 00:40:00,400
Discount works well.

334
00:40:00,720 --> 00:40:11,200
I think my only concern about discount, well, two concerns, obviously, there's a limit to how much you can discount before you're actually kind of losing money or not really making money on those subscribers.

335
00:40:11,680 --> 00:40:20,880
I think as well, it depends on the size of the discount, you know, you might end up having a whole whole lot of low-intention subscribers in your app, which is also not a great long term strategy.

336
00:40:21,440 --> 00:40:25,840
Of course, discounts do tend to work, but these are things that we do need to think about.

337
00:40:26,480 --> 00:40:37,280
The other thing that I think works quite nicely, other than just reminding people of their usage and features, it's also kind of things like reminding people what might be coming up.

338
00:40:37,520 --> 00:40:46,640
So if, for example, you've got a new release coming out, a new feature release, reminding people that that's there and that that's coming up and that they're not going to have access to that too.

339
00:40:47,040 --> 00:40:48,000
can be quite effective.

340
00:40:48,480 --> 00:40:59,760
But I would say that just being careful to balance discounts with making sure that you're actually bringing high intent people back into the app, because obviously that's a longer term strategy.

341
00:41:00,080 --> 00:41:05,280
And also you could segment it by, if you've got enough data, you could segment it by people.

342
00:41:05,920 --> 00:41:18,560
If you understand how many times on average a user renews and you could see somebody who's at the end of that average time, you might be a bit more inclined to offer a discount at that stage.

343
00:41:19,040 --> 00:41:34,400
